==English==

===Etymology===
From Northern {{inh|en|enm|at do|t=to do}}, [[Appendix:Glossary#supine|infinitive]] of {{m|enm|do}}, {{m|enm|don|t=to do}}, see {{m|en|do}}. Influenced by an {{w|Old Norse}} practice of marking the infinitive by using the preposition {{m|non|at}}, {{m|non|att}} (compare {{cog|da|at gå||to go}}). More at {{l|en|at}}, {{l|en|do}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈduː/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-ado.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈdjuː/|a=hypercorrect}}
* {{rhymes|en|uː|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# Trouble; [[troublesome]] business; [[fuss]], [[commotion]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:commotion}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|I|i|text={{smallcaps|Antonio}}:In sooth, I know not why I am so sad.<br>It wearies me; you say it wearies you;<br>But how I caught it, found it, or came by it,<br>What stuff 'tis made of, whereof it is born,<br>I am to learn;<br>And such a wantwit sadness makes of me,<br>That I have much '''ado''' to know myself.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|[[s:Moby-Dick (1851) US edition/Chapter 3|chapter 3]]|passage=But beginning to feel very cold now, half undressed as I was, and remembering what the landlord said about the harpooneer’s not coming home at all that night, it being so very late, I made no more '''ado''', but jumped out of my pantaloons and boots, and then blowing out the light tumbled into bed, and commended myself to the care of heaven.}}
#* {{RQ:William James Varieties|passage=Probably a crab would be filled with a sense of personal outrage if it could hear us class it without '''ado''' or apology as a crustacean, and thus dispose of it. “I am no such thing,” it would say; “I am myself, myself alone.”}}
#* {{see more citations|en}}

====Usage notes====
''Ado'' is mostly used in set phrases, such as ''[[without further ado]]'' or ''[[much ado about nothing]]''.

